  "markdown": "London Heathrow Airport, Terminal 4, airside departures\n\n# Calico Wallpaper airside at Heathrow: a luxury fashion house's store, Terminal 4\n\nA single painted landscape, printed to the store's exact wall sizes, wraps the walls of a luxury fashion house's airside store, hung overnight while the terminal kept running.\n\n![The mural wrapped around an internal corner, full height.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-05-800.webp)\n\n## At a glance\n\nClient, materials & project facts\n\n**Client**\n: A luxury fashion house (name withheld)\n\n**Designer**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Main contractor**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Maker and paper**\n: Calico Wallpaper: bespoke, non-repeating painted-landscape mural, custom-printed\n\n**Area or rooms**\n: –Not recorded\n\n**Scope**\n: Specialist wallcovering installation, overnight, in a live terminal\n\n**Year**\n: 2018\n\n**Delivered in partnership with [Calico Wallpaper](https://calicowallpaper.com/).**\n\n## At a glance\n\n- **For:** a luxury fashion house’s relocated store at London Heathrow, Terminal 4\n- **Where:** airside, in the departures retail area beyond security\n- **When:** the night of 24–25 September 2018; the finished walls were photographed between 02:30 and 03:42\n- **Product:** a bespoke Calico Wallpaper mural. It is a non-repeating, painterly landscape, printed to the store’s exact wall sizes and wrapped continuously around the corners.\n- **Our role:** specialist installation of the Calico panels\n\n## The project\n\nThe brand’s new store concept takes its cue from the painterly British landscape, and in this store the main walls carry it as a single, continuous Calico Wallpaper mural. A band of warm, earthy pink sits over a drifting grey-white mist, which settles into deep ink-blue ground with flecks of green and violet. A lit cove runs along the top, so the mural reads like a horizon floating around the room.\n\nCalico prints murals like this to the exact size of each wall. That makes the work exacting. Every panel has its own place in the picture, so the horizon has to run level and unbroken across each join and around every corner.\n\n## Working airside, in a live terminal\n\nAirport retail is one of the most tightly controlled places we work. What that means in practice:\n\n- **Security first.** Airside work means vetted operatives with airside passes. Tools and materials are checked through security and accounted for on site. Deliveries follow the airport’s logistics and escort procedures.\n- **Overnight working.** In a terminal that never fully closes, the fit-out has to fit around passenger flows. Our photographs of the finished walls were taken between 02:30 and 03:42, with the gate lounges outside empty.\n- **Clean, contained and quiet.** We work inside the hoarding, with protected floors and nothing left outside the unit, so the concourse is ready for the first departures.\n\n## Our approach\n\n- **Panel order decides the picture.** We checked and sequenced the panels so the horizon runs level across the whole store.\n- **The landscape turns the corner without a break.** The mural wraps continuously around internal corners, so the landscape never stops at a wall edge.\n- **Clean lines for the fit-out.** The mural meets a lit cove at the top and the joinery at the base. We left it finished around the services, ready for the shop-fitters’ fixtures and lighting.\n\n*Of these, the overnight working is what our timestamped photographs record for this job. The security and containment points describe what airside work at Heathrow requires in general.*\n\n## The paper\n\nCalico Wallpaper (Brooklyn, New York) makes non-repeating murals from hand-painted artwork, printed to fit each space. This one was bespoke and is not a standard catalogue design. Calico’s custom service is described here:\n\n- [Calico Wallpaper bespoke services](https://calicowallpaper.com/about/bespoke-services/)\n- [Calico Wallpaper collections](https://calicowallpaper.com/collections/)\n\n## Photographs\n\n![Detail of the painted mist band in the Calico mural.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-06-800.webp)\n\n![Detail of the dark ground of the mural wrapping the corner.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-07-800.webp)\n\n![Detail of the mural meeting the lit cove at the top of the wall.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-08-800.webp)\n\n![The long wall of the mural beneath the lit cove.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-04-800.webp)\n\n![The Calico Wallpaper landscape mural running unbroken around a corner of the Terminal 4 store.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-03-800.webp)\n\n![Calico Wallpaper landscape mural across two walls of the store, before the fixtures went in.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-02-800.webp)\n\n![Heathrow Terminal 4 gate lounge, empty in the small hours during the overnight installation.](https://www.mrwallcover.com/media/img/r/heathrow-01-800.webp)\n\nHow the commission ran\n\n## The same standards, on every commission.\n\nWhether the client is a hotel group, a designer or a household, the job runs the same way. These are the parts a photograph never shows.\n\n01\n\n### Survey and scope, room by room\n\nWe measure the wall that will actually be hung, allow for the repeat and the wastage, and write a scope of works room by room before anyone orders paper.\n\n02\n\n### Preparation decides the finish\n\nWalls are filled, made good and primed until they are true. If a wall is not ready for the material specified, we say so before the last drop is ordered.\n\n03\n\n### Protection and containment\n\nFloors, furniture and finished joinery are protected as part of the hang. Work is contained and the place is left tidy each evening. In a home we are visitors; in a hotel we work around the house.\n\n04\n\n### Batch and shade control\n\nDeliveries are checked against the measure, and batches and shading are managed so that a corridor of rooms reads as one surface.\n\n05\n\n### Set out from the room's focal points\n\nPattern is set out from chimneypieces, doorcases and bed walls, so the design meets the cornice and the joinery with composure.\n\n06\n\n### One team, then the return visit\n\nThe people who start are the people who finish. Four to six weeks after completion we come back to look at every seam and edge, and the workmanship is guaranteed for twelve months.\n\n[See the finish up close: seams, internal corners, edges and joins from our own installations](https://www.mrwallcover.com/details/)\n\nBy [Dorin Burcus](https://www.mrwallcover.com/about/#dorin), founder · Last updated 10 October 2026\n",
